  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Equipping Pangolin Range States to Better Implement CITES and Combat Wildlife Trafficking Through Developing Monitoring Methodologies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;The purpose of this project is to equip pangolin range states to better implement CITES and combat wildlife trafficking, through developing species specific monitoring methodologies. This project is intended to conserve pangolins by developing monitoring methods that should enable angolin range states to more effectively determine the status of pangolins populations; to identify priority areas for their conservation; and to better protect populations from wildlife trafficking. Specific activities include: (1) conduct systematic literature reviews on pangolin ecology, biology and natural history and monitoring methods used to date, and on methods used to monitor ecologically similar species; (2) develop a questionnaire on potentially new, existing and adapted methodologies for monitoring pangolin populations to be completed by IUCN SSC Pangolin Specialist Group members and a wide range of biodiversity survey experts globally; (3) using the results of the literature reviews and questionnaire, hold a 3 day workshop to develop methodologies for each of the eight species of pangolin, focusing on (i) being able to determine presence of pangolins at the site level, and (ii) being able to undertake long-term monitoring of populations; (4) produce guidance documents on the methods developed for dissemination to CITES Parties and other stakeholders; (5) document the methods developed in peer-reviewed scientific journal publications; and (6) present the methods developed at the next available CITES meeting.</narrative>
  </description>
